      ""question"": ""What is the differential diagnosis for PPI-refractory heartburn and regurgitation?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Persistent GERD despite inadequate PPI dose/schedule, non-erosive reflux disease, functional heartburn, hiatal hernia, eosinophilic esophagitis, delayed gastric emptying, or inadequate PPI adherence; less commonly, cardiac, pulmonary, or psychiatric causes of chest discomfort"",
      ""rationale"": ""The differential includes both continued acid-mediated GERD (possibly due to suboptimal PPI dosing or timing) and non-GERD etiologies. Eosinophilic esophagitis can mimic GERD and should be considered, especially with nighttime symptoms. Functional disorders may lack objective reflux but present with similar complaints. Other causes of chest discomfort must be considered in refractory cases, although cardiac features are absent here.""
    },
