      ""id"": ""Q2"",
      ""question"": ""Is this condition considered medically urgent or emergent?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Self-care appropriate."",
      ""rationale"": ""The symptoms are transient, reproducible in a specific context, non-progressive, and resolve spontaneously. There are no red flags or systemic findings. No immediate medical intervention is required.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q3"",
      ""question"": ""Which clinical feature most supports the diagnosis of motion sickness over other vestibular disorders?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Temporal relationship of symptoms exclusively to passive motion (car rides) and resolution within minutes of stopping movement."",
      ""rationale"": ""The predictability and context-specificity of symptoms, along with their rapid resolution, differentiate motion sickness from central or peripheral vestibular pathologies, which often persist beyond the provoking stimulus and may be associated with hearing changes or ataxia.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q4"",
      ""question"": ""Are there concerning 'red flag' features that would suggest a central neurological etiology?"",
