      ""rationale"": ""There are no headaches, vomiting, hearing loss, tinnitus, gait instability outside the setting, or abnormal neurological findings on exam—particularly normal cerebellar and cranial nerve testing—which argue against a central lesion.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q5"",
      ""question"": ""Is any further diagnostic testing warranted at this time?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""No."",
      ""rationale"": ""In a child with classic symptoms of motion sickness and a normal neurological and otoscopic exam, further diagnostics such as imaging or audiometry are not indicated and would not change management.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q6"",
      ""question"": ""What non-pharmacological management strategies are most appropriate?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Encourage seated position in the front seat, visual focus on the horizon, use of cool airflow, avoiding reading or screen use during travel, and gradual exposure to travel (habituation)."",
      ""rationale"": ""These strategies are evidence-based and aim to reduce sensory conflict. They are low-risk and often effective, especially in mild cases like this.""
    },
    {
