    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q3"",
      ""question"": ""Which clinical specialty is most appropriate for initial management?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Pediatrics."",
      ""rationale"": ""The patient is a young child presenting with a common pediatric infectious condition. Primary management would fall under general pediatrics, with supportive guidance from primary care providers.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q4"",
      ""question"": ""What are the main differential diagnoses to consider?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Herpangina, herpes simplex virus infection (gingivostomatitis), varicella, rashes associated with streptococcal infection (e.g. scarlet fever), and rashes mimicking HFMD such as erythema multiforme or drug reaction."",
      ""rationale"": ""Herpangina also involves enteroviruses and causes oral ulcers but typically spares the hands and feet. Herpes simplex causes oral ulcers but without characteristic hand-foot vesicles. Varicella presents with a more generalized, polymorphic rash. Scarlet fever may show circumoral pallor and sandpaper rash. Erythema multiforme or drug reactions may mimic vesicular rash but lack the clear infectious cluster history seen here.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q5"",
      ""question"": ""Are there any red flags indicating severe or complicated disease?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""No red flags present at this time."",
      ""rationale"": ""Absence of high fever, dehydration, lethargy, ataxia, seizures, or cardiorespiratory symptoms suggests uncomplicated HFMD. The child’s good hydration, normal mental status, and mild fussiness without pain or refusal to drink indicate a benign course.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q6"",
      ""question"": ""Are diagnostic tests required for confirmation in this case?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""No, clinical diagnosis is sufficient."",
      ""rationale"": ""HFMD is typically diagnosed clinically based on history and characteristic physical findings. Laboratory or imaging tests are not necessary in mild, typical cases and are reserved for atypical or complicated presentations.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q7"",
      ""question"": ""What supportive management should be recommended?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Encourage oral hydration, use of analgesics/antipyretics like acetaminophen or ibuprofen for discomfort, and symptomatic care such as soft, non-acidic foods to minimize oral pain."",
      ""rationale"": ""Management is supportive as HFMD is self-limited. Avoidance of acidic or salty foods helps reduce oral discomfort. Antiviral therapy is not indicated for enteroviruses. Maintaining hydration and comfort are key goals.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q8"",
      ""question"": ""What infection control and public health measures should be advised?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Keep the child home from daycare until fever has resolved and vesicles have dried; reinforce hand hygiene and surface disinfection to limit transmission."",
      ""rationale"": ""HFMD is highly contagious via fecal-oral and respiratory routes. Children are most infectious during the first week of illness. Public health guidance recommends exclusion from childcare until afebrile and no open or weeping lesions.""
    }
  ],
