Traumatic anuria, initial encounter;Crushing injuries involving multiple regions of lower limb(s);Crush injury/rhabdomyolysis;A 40-year-old male construction worker is extricated by rescue crews after being trapped under a collapsed concrete wall at a building site for approximately 4 hours. Both lower extremities were pinned. Upon extrication, he is alert and reports severe pain in both legs and a sensation of numbness. He has no significant past medical history. On examination, both lower extremities are markedly swollen, tense, and ecchymotic from the thighs to the ankles. Vital signs: T 36.4C, HR 118, RR 24, BP 96/62, SpO2 95%. Pedal pulses are weak bilaterally. Sensation is diminished throughout both legs. He reports dark brown urine since a Foley catheter was placed in the field, with only 50 mL output over the past 2 hours. Labs drawn immediately: potassium 6.8 mEq/L, creatinine 3.4 mg/dL, CK 124,000 U/L, phosphorus 7.6 mg/dL, calcium 6.2 mg/dL, uric acid 14.2 mg/dL, myoglobin 48,000 ng/mL, bicarbonate 14 mEq/L, lactate 6.8 mmol/L. ECG shows peaked T waves. Aggressive IV normal saline resuscitation at 1.5 L/hr with sodium bicarbonate supplementation is initiated, along with continuous cardiac monitoring and IV calcium gluconate for cardioprotection.;;;;;40 y/o m construction worker, trapped under debris 4 hrs, bilateral LE crush injury, dark urine, CK 124000, K+ 6.8, Cr 3.4, myoglobin 48000, oliguric, peaked T waves on ECG, aggressive fluid resuscitation initiated;Requires emergent care;;AI;Emergency;young age (25-44);"""role_name"": ""Senior general diagnostician"",
