      ""id"": ""Q1"",
      ""question"": ""What is the most likely diagnosis based on the clinical presentation and gestational context?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Severe placental abruption with suspected maternal-fetal compromise and developing disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)."",
      ""rationale"": ""The triad of sudden painful vaginal bleeding, a firm/woody uterus, and severe abdominal pain at 34 weeks in a patient with prior abruption and chronic hypertension is classic for placental abruption. Fetal distress (tachycardia, absent variability, late decelerations), maternal tachycardia, hypotension, and anemia support hypovolemic shock and fetal compromise. Laboratory findings (low fibrinogen, elevated D-dimer, prolonged PT, thrombocytopenia) indicate coagulopathy consistent with DIC, a known complication of abruption.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q2"",
      ""question"": ""What features indicate the urgency of the clinical situation?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Presence of fetal bradycardia/tachycardia with absent variability and late decelerations, maternal hemodynamic instability (HR 124, BP 96/62), significant anemia, ongoing bleeding, and signs of consumptive coagulopathy indicate imminent maternal and fetal deterioration requiring immediate delivery."",
      ""rationale"": ""The combination of fetal heart rate abnormalities consistent with late-stage fetal compromise, ongoing maternal bleeding, signs of shock, and coagulopathy suggest both life-threatening fetal distress and progression toward irreversible maternal coagulopathy if not delivered emergently.""
    },
