      ""id"": ""Q3"",
      ""question"": ""What clinical finding most strongly suggests disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Low fibrinogen (142 mg/dL) and markedly elevated D-dimer (>20,000 ng/mL) with thrombocytopenia and prolonged PT."",
      ""rationale"": ""In the setting of acute hemorrhage and placental abruption, a drop in fibrinogen is an early and sensitive marker of DIC. The elevated D-dimer reflects extensive fibrinolysis, while progressive thrombocytopenia and PT prolongation confirm ongoing consumptive coagulopathy.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q4"",
      ""question"": ""Why is this patient's history of chronic hypertension and prior abruption clinically significant?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Chronic hypertension and a prior abruption are the two strongest risk factors for recurrence and severity of abruption in the current pregnancy."",
      ""rationale"": ""Chronic hypertension increases the risk of vascular compromise of the placenta. Prior abruption significantly elevates the recurrence risk to ~10–15%, indicating a predisposition likely due to underlying placental vasculopathy.""
    },
