      ""rationale"": ""Although the Seidel test is negative and no foreign body is visible, high-velocity metal injury raises concern for occult globe penetration or intraocular foreign body. Traumatic iritis can coexist with corneal abrasion and may present with photophobia and decreased vision. Infectious keratitis must be ruled out, especially if there is any delay in healing or worsening signs.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q5"",
      ""question"": ""Are there any red flags present that suggest a more serious underlying condition?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Yes, history of high-velocity metal trauma without eye protection is a red flag for occult penetrating injury or retained intraocular metallic foreign body."",
      ""rationale"": ""High-speed metal grinding creates significant risk for intraocular foreign body even if no retained material is visible externally. Such injuries may not have obvious signs (like hypopyon, hyphema, or positive Seidel test) initially, and imaging (e.g., CT or MRI if no metal suspected) may be required. Delayed diagnosis can lead to endophthalmitis or permanent vision loss.""
