Penalize if non-accidental trauma is not considered at all in a pediatric scald injury history.";"Vignette:
An 8-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department after pulling a pot of boiling water off the stove onto himself approximately 45 minutes ago. He sustained scald burns to his anterior chest, abdomen, both upper extremities, and portions of his face. He has been crying inconsolably. He has no significant past medical history. On examination, he is alert but in severe pain and distress. Vital signs: T 36.6C, HR 152, RR 28, BP 94/58, SpO2 98%. Weight is 28 kg. Burns cover approximately 20% total body surface area: areas of erythema with large intact and ruptured blisters (superficial partial thickness) on the chest and abdomen, with waxy white insensate areas (deep partial to full thickness) on both forearms. No circumferential burns are identified. Facial burns are superficial with no singed nasal hairs and no evidence of inhalation injury. He has no hoarseness or stridor. Parkland formula is calculated (20% TBSA x 28 kg x 4 mL = 2,240 mL LR over first 24 hours), IV access is obtained, and morphine is administered for pain.";openai_compatible;gpt:///deepseek-v4-flash/latest;Default;You are a careful expert assistant. Follow the requested format exactly. Do not use information not grounded in the provided case.;;;50.0;0.0;;703.0;383.0;1086.0;False;;;;;;;;;;;;openai_compatible;gpt:///gpt-oss-120b/latest;Default;You are a careful expert assistant. Follow the requested format exactly. Do not use information not grounded in the provided case.;;;50.0;0.0;;774.0;3000.0;3774.0;False;;;;;;;;;;;;openai_compatible;gpt:///qwen3-235b-a22b-fp8/latest;Default;You are a careful expert assistant. Follow the requested format exactly. Do not use information not grounded in the provided case.;Requires emergent care;General Surgery;0.0;8.0;"{
