A 28-year-old woman with known epilepsy is brought in by ambulance after her roommate witnessed continuous generalized tonic-clonic seizure activity for approximately 12 minutes. EMS administered 10 mg of intramuscular midazolam en route, but seizure activity persisted. Her past medical history includes focal epilepsy diagnosed at age 16, treated with levetiracetam. Her roommate reports she recently missed several doses of medication due to insurance issues. On arrival, she remains in continuous generalized tonic-clonic seizure. Vital signs: T 39.2C, HR 142, RR (irregular, 8-30), BP 168/98, SpO2 88% on non-rebreather mask. She is cyanotic around the lips. Finger-stick glucose is 142 mg/dL. IV lorazepam 4 mg is administered with brief cessation of convulsive activity, but seizures resume within 2 minutes.";openai_compatible;gpt:///deepseek-v4-flash/latest;Default;You are a careful expert assistant. Follow the requested format exactly. Do not use information not grounded in the provided case.;;;50.0;0.0;;640.0;3000.0;3640.0;False;;;;;;;;;;;;openai_compatible;gpt:///gpt-oss-120b/latest;Default;You are a careful expert assistant. Follow the requested format exactly. Do not use information not grounded in the provided case.;;;50.0;0.0;;702.0;2684.0;3386.0;False;;;;;;;;;;;;openai_compatible;gpt:///qwen3-235b-a22b-fp8/latest;Default;You are a careful expert assistant. Follow the requested format exactly. Do not use information not grounded in the provided case.;Requires emergent care;Neurology;0.0;8.0;"{
  ""sub_questions"": [
    {
      ""id"": ""Q1"",
      ""question"": ""Is this a case of generalized convulsive status epilepticus?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Yes"",
      ""rationale"": ""The patient has continuous generalized tonic-clonic seizure activity lasting over 12 minutes, persisting despite administration of intramuscular midazolam and a brief response to IV lorazepam before recurrence—meeting clinical criteria for generalized convulsive status epilepticus (GCSE), which is typically defined as continuous seizure activity >5 minutes or recurrent seizures without return to baseline.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q2"",
      ""question"": ""What is the level of urgency in this clinical scenario?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Immediate life-threatening emergency"",
      ""rationale"": ""Generalized convulsive status epilepticus is associated with high morbidity and mortality if not rapidly treated; prolonged seizures can lead to neurologic injury, hyperthermia, metabolic derangements, rhabdomyolysis, and cardiorespiratory compromise, all of which are evident in this patient (e.g., hypoxia, tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia).""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q3"",
      ""question"": ""Which specialty is most immediately required for management?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Neurology"",
      ""rationale"": ""While initial resuscitation is managed in the emergency department, neurologic expertise is essential for diagnosis confirmation, escalation of antiepileptic therapy, EEG monitoring, and determining underlying cause; status epilepticus warrants urgent neurologic consultation and intervention.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q4"",
      ""question"": ""What is the most likely precipitant of this episode?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Medication non-adherence due to missed levetiracetam doses"",
      ""rationale"": ""The roommate reports recent missed doses of levetiracetam because of insurance issues, which is a well-known and common trigger for breakthrough seizures in patients with known epilepsy, especially in those with prior history of seizure control on medication.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q5"",
      ""question"": ""Are there any red flags suggesting complications or secondary causes of status epilepticus?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Yes"",
      ""rationale"": ""Hyperthermia (39.2°C), tachycardia, hypoxia (SpO2 88% despite high-flow O2), and cyanosis are red flags indicating physiological instability and potential secondary complications of prolonged seizures (e.g., neurogenic hyperthermia, respiratory depression, aspiration risk); though glucose is normal, exclusion of infectious, metabolic, or structural etiologies is needed.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q6"",
      ""question"": ""What immediate diagnostic steps are indicated?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Rapid neurological assessment, continuous pulse oximetry, cardiac monitoring, labs (CBC, electrolytes, glucose, renal/hepatic function, antiepileptic drug levels, toxicology screen), and early neuroimaging and EEG"",
      ""rationale"": ""After securing airway, breathing, and circulation, diagnostics should focus on reversible causes; given the patient’s altered mental status and ongoing seizures, urgent head imaging (to exclude structural cause) and EEG (to confirm ongoing seizure activity and guide treatment) are necessary.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q7"",
      ""question"": ""What is the next immediate management step after failure of first-line benzodiazepines?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Administration of second-line IV antiseizure medication (e.g., fosphenytoin, valproate, levetiracetam) and preparation for possible ICU admission with consideration of anesthesia if refractory"",
      ""rationale"": ""The patient had only transient response to IV lorazepam (first-line benzodiazepine), indicating treatment failure; guidelines recommend prompt administration of second-line agents; simultaneous airway protection, hemodynamic support, and escalation to an ICU setting should be anticipated.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q8"",
      ""question"": ""Is airway compromise evident and does it require intervention?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Yes, urgent airway intervention should be strongly considered"",
      ""rationale"": ""The patient is hypoxic (SpO2 88% on non-rebreather), cyanotic, and has irregular respirations, suggesting alveolar hypoventilation from prolonged seizure activity or post-ictal depression; risk of aspiration is high, and early intubation may be necessary to secure oxygenation and protect the airway.""
    }
  ],
