    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q3"",
      ""question"": ""What specialty should primarily manage this case upon stabilization?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Endocrinology"",
      ""rationale"": ""The core issue is acute adrenal crisis in a patient with known Addison disease, which is an endocrine emergency. Endocrinology is best equipped to manage long-term replacement, dosing adjustments, and patient education on sick-day rules.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q4"",
      ""question"": ""What are the critical differential diagnoses that must be considered?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Sepsis, acute gastroenteritis with severe dehydration, acute kidney injury, and hyperkalemic cardiac arrhythmia"",
      ""rationale"": ""The patient has fever, vomiting, diarrhea, and hypotension, which overlap with sepsis and severe dehydration. However, the extreme electrolyte disturbances, hypoglycemia, low cortisol, and history of adrenal insufficiency point most strongly to adrenal crisis. Sepsis may be a trigger but is not the primary diagnosis. Hyperkalemia poses an immediate cardiac threat and must be addressed.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q5"",
      ""question"": ""What red flags indicate this is more than a typical gastroenteritis?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Hypotension unresponsive to IV fluids, severe hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypoglycemia, mental status changes (lethargy), and very low cortisol level"",
      ""rationale"": ""These findings are inconsistent with simple viral gastroenteritis and indicate a systemic metabolic/endocrine crisis. The lack of response to 2L NS, neurologic compromise, and lab extremes signal adrenal crisis or another life-threatening condition.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q6"",
      ""question"": ""What additional diagnostics are urgently needed beyond initial labs?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Repeat electrolytes, glucose, renal function, serum cortisol (already obtained), ACTH (to confirm primary adrenal insufficiency), and continuous ECG monitoring"",
      ""rationale"": ""Ongoing monitoring of sodium, potassium, glucose, and renal function is essential. ACTH will help confirm primary Addison disease. Continuous ECG is needed due to hyperkalemia with peaked T waves to detect progression to arrhythmia.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q7"",
      ""question"": ""What immediate therapeutic steps are needed after initial stress-dose hydrocortisone?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Repeat IV hydrocortisone every 6–8 hours, IV saline resuscitation with dextrose-containing fluids, and emergency treatment for hyperkalemia (e.g., calcium gluconate, insulin-glucose, albuterol, sodium bicarbonate if indicated)"",
      ""rationale"": ""Stress-dose glucocorticoids are first-line and have been started. However, prolonged replacement dosing is required. IV fluid resuscitation must continue. Dextrose-containing fluids (e.g., D5NS) should be used due to hyponatremia and hypoglycemia. Hyperkalemia with ECG changes requires immediate intervention to prevent cardiac arrest.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q8"",
      ""question"": ""Is this patient’s neurological status (lethargy) likely secondary to the endocrine emergency?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Yes"",
      ""rationale"": ""Lethargy in the context of hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, hypotension, and adrenal insufficiency is likely due to the metabolic derangements of adrenal crisis. Hypoglycemia alone can cause encephalopathy, and cerebral edema from severe hyponatremia may contribute. No focal neurological signs are noted, supporting systemic rather than primary neurological etiology.""
    }
