    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q3"",
      ""question"": ""Which medical specialty is best suited to manage this condition if prescription treatment or further evaluation is needed?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Dermatology."",
      ""rationale"": ""Tinea pedis is a cutaneous fungal infection, and dermatologists specialize in diagnosing and managing skin conditions, including difficult or recalcitrant cases. However, general medicine providers can also manage typical cases.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q4"",
      ""question"": ""What are the key differential diagnoses to consider in a young adult with interdigital foot scaling and maceration?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""Contact dermatitis, erythrasma, psoriasis, and candidal intertrigo."",
      ""rationale"": ""Contact dermatitis may present with peeling and itch but is often more eczematous and may involve broader areas. Erythrasma, caused by Corynebacterium minutissimum, appears as reddish-brown, slightly scaling patches in intertriginous areas and fluoresces coral-red under Wood’s lamp. Psoriasis can involve the feet but usually presents with thicker plaques and may affect nails. Candidal intertrigo is less common but may occur in macerated, warm environments and is more likely in immunocompromised or moist-prone individuals.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q5"",
      ""question"": ""Are there any red flags present suggesting a more serious or systemic condition?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""No red flags present."",
      ""rationale"": ""There is no pain, ulceration, bullae, lymphangitis, fever, or toenail involvement. The patient is immunocompetent, without diabetes, and the lesion is limited to interdigital areas. These factors suggest a benign, localized infection.""
    },
    {
      ""id"": ""Q6"",
      ""question"": ""What diagnostic tests, if any, are warranted in this case?"",
      ""my_answer"": ""KOH microscopy of skin scrapings could confirm fungal hyphae, but is not routinely necessary in classic presentations."",
      ""rationale"": ""In typical clinical cases with characteristic findings, diagnosis is clinical. KOH preparation increases diagnostic accuracy when uncertainty exists. Culture or Wood’s lamp may help differentiate from erythrasma or candidiasis but are not required here initially.""
    },
    {
